## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a simple data structure with guaranteed O(1) operations and handle edge cases like duplicates and emptiness.

## Key Concepts

- Two-stack technique to keep current minimum per depth
- Constant-time top and getMin
- Correct empty-state handling and error signaling

## Code Example

```javascript
class MinStack {
  constructor() {
    this.stack = [];
    this.mins = [];
  }
  push(x) {
    this.stack.push(x);
    if (this.mins.length === 0) {
      this.mins.push(x);
    } else {
      this.mins.push(Math.min(x, this.mins[this.mins.length - 1]));
    }
  }
  pop() {
    if (this.stack.length === 0) throw new Error('empty');
    this.stack.pop();
    this.mins.pop();
  }
  top() {
    if (this.stack.length === 0) throw new Error('empty');
    return this.stack[this.stack.length - 1];
  }
  getMin() {
    if (this.mins.length === 0) throw new Error('empty');
    return this.mins[this.mins.length - 1];
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend to also support getMax() efficiently? 
- How would you optimize memory if input values are highly repetitive?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of maintaining an ordered dynamic sequence with logarithmic updates while augmenting nodes with aggregate data for fast queries.

## Key Concepts
- Implicit treap for sequence indexing
- Split by position and merge operations
- Augmented data: size and sum to support rangeSum
- Handling duplicates via node values and randomized priorities

## Code Example
```javascript
class Node { constructor(val){ this.val=val; this.pri=Math.random(); this.left=null; this.right=null; this.sz=1; this.sum=val; } }
function sz(n){ return n? n.sz:0; }
function sum(n){ return n? n.sum:0; }
function upd(n){ if(n){ n.sz = 1 + sz(n.left) + sz(n.right); n.sum = n.val + sum(n.left) + sum(n.right); } }
function split(root, key){ // first key elements go to left
  if(!root) return [null,null];
  if(sz(root.left) >= key){ const [l,r] = split(root.left, key); root.left = r; upd(root); return [l, root]; }
  else { const [l,r] = split(root.right, key - sz(root.left) - 1); root.right = l; upd(root); return [root, r]; }
}
function merge(a,b){ if(!a||!b) return a||b; if(a.pri < b.pri){ a.right = merge(a.right, b); upd(a); return a; } else { b.left = merge(a, b.left); upd(b); return b; } }
function insertAt(root, index, value){ const node = new Node(value); const [L,R] = split(root, index); return merge(merge(L, node), R); }
function eraseAt(root, index){ const [L, R] = split(root, index); const [M, RR] = split(R, 1); return merge(L, RR); }
function rangeSumQuery(root, l, r){ const [A, BR] = split(root, l); const [B, C] = split(BR, r - l + 1); const res = sum(B); const newRoot = merge(A, merge(B, C)); return {root: newRoot, sum: res}; }
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests knowledge of advanced balanced BST variants and in-place sequence augmentation. It also checks correctness of gcd propagation through splits and merges and edge handling for empty or single-element ranges.

## Key Concepts

- Implicit indexing via size augmentation
- Subtree gcd maintenance and gcd associative property
- Safe split/merge to isolate segments
- Handling duplicates and non-contiguous edits efficiently

## Code Example

```javascript
class Node { constructor(val){ this.val=val; this.prio=Math.random(); this.l=null; this.r=null; this.sz=1; this.gcd=val; } }
function sz(n){return n? n.sz:0}
function gcd(a,b){ while(b){ let t=a%b; a=b; b=t;} return Math.abs(a); }
function upd(n){ if(!n) return; n.sz = 1 + sz(n.l) + sz(n.r); const gl = n.l? n.l.gcd:0; const gr = n.r? n.r.gcd:0; n.gcd = gcd(gcd(gl, n.val), gr); }
function split(n, k){ if(!n) return [null, null]; if(sz(n.l) >= k){ const [a,b] = split(n.l, k); n.l=b; upd(n); return [a, n]; } else { const [a,b] = split(n.r, k - sz(n.l) -1); n.r=a; upd(n); return [n, b]; } }
function merge(a,b){ if(!a||!b) return a||b; if(a.prio < b.prio){ a.r = merge(a.r, b); upd(a); return a; } else { b.l = merge(a, b.l); upd(b); return b; } }
// Insert, erase, rangeGCD usage explained in prose
```

